Options A and C have been discussed in the final paragraph - {"The crux of the Biden administration’s plan for a just transition thus far is a policy granting tax incentives to businesses investing in “energy communities.”... But incentives are, well, just incentives."}
Option B: The author notes that the Biden administration has acknowledged the lack of investment in industrial and place-based policies and is now diverting funds into these: {"The problem is threefold. First, the United States does not invest heavily in industrial policy or place-based policy compared with some of its rich-country peers, though the Biden administration has started to push billions of dollars into both."} Therefore, it's incorrect to cite Option B as a criticism of the Biden administration's just-transition policy. 
Option D can be understood from the following excerpt - {"...the Republican Party tends to reject the premise that the country needs to move away from fossil-fuel production at all, making them a weak partner in setting up just-transition plans..."}
Hence, Option B is the correct choice.
